#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124466 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dbd6ccf-ed91-536c-bc41-cccc05325c4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409541} {:answer/id #uuid "2ff6ca25-dc52-5f06-b4e5-fccf5f8c424e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409542} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5e8d7a-13cb-5c4a-af4c-8873999183d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409544} {:answer/id #uuid "98963520-6ef4-5229-b4a2-12278d12f050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409549} {:answer/id #uuid "feeb48aa-dc46-5157-aa75-f72619f1adb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2dbd6ccf-ed91-536c-bc41-cccc05325c4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409541} :answer-field/id #uuid "90260639-fefe-562c-8509-7dc58803f6c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409554}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "538fdcf0-8dcd-525c-becd-0317742dbf1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-72010" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)={e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}?$\n\nI. Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,-1)$\n\nII. ${f}_{y}(-1,-1)$ is defined\n\nIII. ${f}_{x}(0,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\cdot \\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{xy}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2\\sqrt{xy}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\cdot \\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{xy}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2\\sqrt{xy}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one. \n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(1,-1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,-1)={e}^{\\sqrt{(1)(-1)}}=-{e}^{\\sqrt{-1}}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have a negative number under the radical.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(-1,-1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(-1,-1)={e}^{\\sqrt{(-1)(-1)}}={e}^{\\sqrt{1}}={e}^{1}=e,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-1,-1) & =\\frac{(-1){e}^{\\sqrt{(-1)(-1)}}}{2\\sqrt{(-1)(-1)}}=-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{1}}}{2\\sqrt{1}}=-\\frac{e}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(0,1)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,1)=1\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{0\\cdot 1}}}{2\\sqrt{0\\cdot 1}}=\\frac{{e}^{0}}{2\\sqrt{0}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II  only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409584 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059689 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d56b033-a2c8-57c7-adde-e574d726ec78" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Symbolic Conditional Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "776196c9-cd05-5240-9195-0b0ce27cb444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409574} {:answer/id #uuid "8c6f81fa-ef3e-50ce-8cd2-a95e102cf1da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409575} {:answer/id #uuid "9ebe90ec-2497-5de0-b5bd-ba3d0c79d56c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409576} {:answer/id #uuid "cff472a8-e087-557d-80e6-5d535af2d5c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409578} {:answer/id #uuid "e1eca4c9-2b1c-5f16-824c-d7eea7cfd316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409580}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "776196c9-cd05-5240-9195-0b0ce27cb444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409574} :answer-field/id #uuid "7645b3d9-5bcf-5720-89a4-adc2f8073a1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1308109d-5efe-51ad-bfa1-4050522344dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-182506" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A⇒¬B≡¬(A∧B)$\n- $¬(A⇒¬B)≡A∧B$\n- $¬(A⇒¬B)≡¬A∧B$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A⇒¬B$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬A∨¬B,$ which by De Morgan's law is equivalent to $¬(A∧B).$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. From part I, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nA⇒¬B≡¬(A∧B)\n$$\n\nBy negating this statement, we get\n\n$$\n¬(A⇒¬B)≡¬(¬(A∧B)).\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n\n$$\nA∧B.\n$$\n\nSo, $¬(A⇒¬B)$ is equivalent to $A∧B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409888 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092339 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd399539-33cf-5738-b970-fcc6c1d919c8" :knowledge-point/title "Using Properties of Indicator Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14622146-9357-5002-bb58-2d5df436755d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409842} {:answer/id #uuid "3e0876cb-849b-532e-9b46-8b0f0fdbeb1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409851} {:answer/id #uuid "6ee506d6-f345-54fa-9845-d354cdc4d92e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409859} {:answer/id #uuid "b3660aee-3664-5309-b332-b14dc695c7ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409868} {:answer/id #uuid "d0708573-229b-5e9d-a525-9d080007c898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3660aee-3664-5309-b332-b14dc695c7ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409868} :answer-field/id #uuid "924887b9-029f-5ae2-880f-5a02a4c501f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09ca95e9-e7fd-5d14-80c3-ab64bc50a23d" :question/math-academy-id "q-174385" :question/problem "Given that ${1}_{A}(q)=0,$ which of the following statements about $\\overset{―}{A}$ are true?\n\nI. ${1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(q)=1$\n\nII. ${1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(q)=0$\n\nIII. $q\\in \\overset{―}{A}$\n\n*Hint: ${1}_{A}$ denotes the indicator function of the set $A$ and ${1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}$ denotes the indicator function of the complement of $A.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(q) & =1-{1}_{A}(q) \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(q)=1,$ we conclude that $q$ belongs to $\\overset{―}{A}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409890 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092339 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd399539-33cf-5738-b970-fcc6c1d919c8" :knowledge-point/title "Using Properties of Indicator Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43e6f09d-b15b-583f-b322-ed2cb2110fd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409853} {:answer/id #uuid "4dbbb73e-91a8-55fd-a1de-9d7b3f028b3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409855} {:answer/id #uuid "a8709f24-d2a8-5df8-9964-5dbb01efd971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409866} {:answer/id #uuid "fa079c9e-2ffa-5770-8242-48897473068d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409878} {:answer/id #uuid "fb44f87f-b177-51f3-96ce-7df17d89d7db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dbbb73e-91a8-55fd-a1de-9d7b3f028b3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409855} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f6ddc19-1832-5d0d-a446-4a288e0c42e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c60efa7-9f70-50c9-8725-0334784ed791" :question/math-academy-id "q-174402" :question/problem "$$\n{1}_{A}(t)=1,\\,{1}_{B}(t)=1\n$$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(t)=1$\n\nII. ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(t)=0$\n\nIII. $t\\in A\\cup B$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(t) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(t),{1}_{B}(t)\\} \\\\  & =max\\{1,1\\} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(t)=1,$ we conclude that $t$ belongs to $A\\cup B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409891 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092339 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd399539-33cf-5738-b970-fcc6c1d919c8" :knowledge-point/title "Using Properties of Indicator Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a0cc322-449d-5a1c-a8d7-1039e1ba0529" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409845} {:answer/id #uuid "7bdeb40f-7a93-51cd-bc84-fa02eb15dc72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409860} {:answer/id #uuid "9366fcca-3b9b-567c-9045-3a0693b79cf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409862} {:answer/id #uuid "de642bb2-a99e-53bd-8f67-e1c68961beb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409874} {:answer/id #uuid "f75b591c-4385-5f6c-a77e-5445cd4d5bf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bdeb40f-7a93-51cd-bc84-fa02eb15dc72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409860} :answer-field/id #uuid "652fe437-abe7-5a5a-bb3a-38f7c9e05f8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92b8a61e-f7a9-5e1e-adba-90e2fa6c6243" :question/math-academy-id "q-174394" :question/problem "$$\n{1}_{A}(-1)=1,\\,{1}_{B}(-1)=1\n$$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(-1)=1$\n\nII. ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(-1)=0$\n\nIII. $-1\\in A\\cap B$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(-1) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(-1),{1}_{B}(-1)\\} \\\\  & =min\\{1,1\\} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(-1)=1,$ we conclude that $-1$ belongs to $A\\cap B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092339 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd399539-33cf-5738-b970-fcc6c1d919c8" :knowledge-point/title "Using Properties of Indicator Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06dd1182-e612-5525-b9de-c12d2d05c3e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409840} {:answer/id #uuid "4068f7e2-a0ab-5191-84b6-5c4067339274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409852} {:answer/id #uuid "618b2a8e-572b-5ad5-b3c0-c1a42f8e3e99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409856} {:answer/id #uuid "a84e75b5-3a80-5752-b9ec-b71303a5aec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409865} {:answer/id #uuid "e97f4d7f-aabf-5567-8431-321ca8787569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a84e75b5-3a80-5752-b9ec-b71303a5aec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409865} :answer-field/id #uuid "83594dda-c004-5625-a3b0-3d790a7941a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8a05322-998a-5d01-8b89-68a8bc93dd0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-174380" :question/problem "Given that ${1}_{A}(0)=0,$ which of the following statements about $\\overset{―}{A}$ are true?\n\nI. ${1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(0)=1$\n\nII. ${1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(0)=0$\n\nIII. $0\\in \\overset{―}{A}$\n\n*Hint: ${1}_{A}$ denotes the indicator function of the set $A$ and ${1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}$ denotes the indicator function of the complement of $A.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(0) & =1-{1}_{A}(0) \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(0)=1,$ we conclude that $0$ belongs to $\\overset{―}{A}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01ad9700-5aaa-5b00-8d2f-acce6ed59c9a" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "179020df-9cb4-5bfd-b5d3-c46b461692c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409937} {:answer/id #uuid "2d9d0d51-0d80-5463-9667-8ab96e0500d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409946} {:answer/id #uuid "7284a027-a46c-51c1-b523-0dd744da21f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409957} {:answer/id #uuid "adc24338-1549-5879-8c00-138c6449cd32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409980} {:answer/id #uuid "ec35298f-a284-5478-97e5-643be853c7a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409989}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "179020df-9cb4-5bfd-b5d3-c46b461692c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409937} :answer-field/id #uuid "d92eaa22-5617-5adb-9aa6-252e76f8012f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a60aead-59e2-5a19-8c88-b84da12bb8f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-90823" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)=2-{x}^{2}?$\n\nI. The range of $f$ is $(-\\infty ,-2]$\n\nII. $∀\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,2],$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$\n\nIII. $f$ maps $R$ **into** $R$" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/35/3575afe4fd4e5aa6754496154986b272a43fb5c035ec8d69621d52fad16ee1ea.png)\n\nRecall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. From the graph, the highest $y$-value is $2,$ and it occurs at the point $(0,2).$ The graph will cover all $y$-values lower than $y=2$, including $y=2$ itself. So the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,2].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,2]$, then for all $y$ contained within $(-\\infty ,2],$ there exists some $x$ contained within $R$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x$ such that $f(x)=3\\in R.$ So, $f$ maps $R$ *into* $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410009 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01ad9700-5aaa-5b00-8d2f-acce6ed59c9a" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ee280af-f80a-5d65-a83c-f42a485b1769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409933} {:answer/id #uuid "68a33e1c-563b-5afc-b17f-ce5f5348dc03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409955} {:answer/id #uuid "97ad711b-b0d6-57f1-b652-f7af62d44cc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409968} {:answer/id #uuid "9d93993b-037d-5b0b-9adb-08cfead1e626" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409973} {:answer/id #uuid "bfa6a430-3aaf-57ed-b195-d170259f40e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfa6a430-3aaf-57ed-b195-d170259f40e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409986} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ef56aef-eb30-5217-a5bd-4df99c5c1ffa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409996}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "39b57e8a-5e0f-5758-8d6b-0d9667e7e35b" :question/math-academy-id "q-105258" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)={2}^{-x}?$\n\nI. The range of $f$ is $[0,\\infty )$\n\nII. $∀\\,y\\in R,$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$\n\nIII. $f$ maps $R$ **into** $R$" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/c7/c79c1123036c978c8c4da514651c0bb1c8e3df34c8bbaff099bee601d568ed10.png)\n\nRecall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The graph will cover all $y$-values greater than $y=0$, excluding $y=0$ itself. So the range of the function is $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is false. Take, for example, $y=-1.$ Since the range of the function is $(0,\\infty ),$ there can't be an $x\\in R$ whose image is $-1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, the function is not a surjection.  So, $f$ maps $R$ *into* $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01ad9700-5aaa-5b00-8d2f-acce6ed59c9a" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0af2f699-18f8-5488-b57d-5bedd5d91355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409930} {:answer/id #uuid "2e30104c-6d17-5f7e-b442-7081b80f61f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409947} {:answer/id #uuid "85a12ed4-f1bb-509b-a022-94454c885ce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409962} {:answer/id #uuid "85ad4409-68ef-5896-9ea7-15e84a8aaa89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409963} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac9a873-d027-576c-98d9-929e1a3a1dde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85a12ed4-f1bb-509b-a022-94454c885ce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409962} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bacaf0b-030b-541e-8b85-79bfadfb698c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4110bb56-279f-50bf-96a1-301ebf1eb3ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-90859" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:(0,\\infty )\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)?$\n\nI. The range of $f$ is $(0,\\infty )$\n\nII. $∀\\,y\\in R$ $∃\\,x\\in (0,\\infty )$ such that $f(x)=y$\n\nIII. $f$ maps $(0,\\infty )$ **into** $R$" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/5e/5e3e074a2d029aeb3f4016332b9fb762fff6ec60ed7dce0290f97a95d6cb57c1.png)\n\nRecall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. From the graph, there is no highest or lowest $y$ value. The graph will cover any real value $y.$ So the range of the function is all $R..$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\infty )$, then for all $y$ contained within $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ there exists some $x$ contained within $R$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\infty )$ the function is a surjection. Therefore, $f$ is *not* an into function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd378899-03ff-57a0-848c-11b93c8e5055" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is a Real Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "230a40a8-f500-5f2f-a709-31d71dbc148c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409941} {:answer/id #uuid "24207a50-894d-5448-94b0-920d3599e670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409943} {:answer/id #uuid "87b5a6e9-7f66-50a5-a1c5-4245ae5ae0b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409964} {:answer/id #uuid "94b4368d-14b9-539d-b55c-e8ad24e70c97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409967} {:answer/id #uuid "e18b5536-8d8b-5756-98f0-ccf4338f0e33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24207a50-894d-5448-94b0-920d3599e670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409943} :answer-field/id #uuid "575d2b88-29b9-5fe3-81fe-8497081bd470" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b62af08-9109-5599-b006-f2ceb899c9fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-105263" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to (-\\infty ,1)$ which is given by $f(x)=1-{2}^{x}?$\n\nI. The range of $f$ is $(-\\infty ,1)$\n\nII. $∀\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,1],$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$\n\nIII. $f$ is a function of $R$ **into** $(-\\infty ,1)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/35/35bd21eef06d1ea63aba6e420d9842de61f9248c4fa26c2895f6825f4e7a3848.png)\n\nRecall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the function takes all $y$-values lower than $y=1$, excluding $y=1$ itself. So the range of the function is indeed $(-\\infty ,1).$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,1)$,  there can't be an $x\\in R$ whose image is $1\\notin (-\\infty ,1).$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement I is true, the function is a surjection.  So, $f$ is *not* a function of $R$ *into* $(-\\infty ,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410545 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a5d11c2-4a7c-5fdc-a363-8a87b90db19e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8404cbe2-ec28-5347-a546-978b108f6afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410527} {:answer/id #uuid "ae69705d-4393-52b2-abc9-696b673d6331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410531} {:answer/id #uuid "e419addd-03bc-5503-b8f2-4323b5a419e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410536} {:answer/id #uuid "e6ceea94-487e-5a46-b3ea-38f442055374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410537} {:answer/id #uuid "e773efeb-2aed-5d30-a63f-618047d3e38e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8404cbe2-ec28-5347-a546-978b108f6afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410527} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc8cd6e0-fec4-59f6-bf63-f95c9e73406a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a087057-8a12-50fd-9bce-b13921276b0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-159782" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$\n\nII. $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w\\})=2$\n\nIII. $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=2$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement II is true. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w\\}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ only. So, $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w\\})=2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=3$ (not $2$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411065 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059689 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d56b033-a2c8-57c7-adde-e574d726ec78" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Symbolic Conditional Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "636ebc41-322a-50d9-8335-010fc61f221b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411014} {:answer/id #uuid "79ead6e4-4892-5762-bdb7-b1413f5768d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411017} {:answer/id #uuid "7d0752bb-bd77-5627-a153-d3ceecd28fb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411020} {:answer/id #uuid "a98771cb-023d-5e3f-bb77-fb7e1b8058aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411032} {:answer/id #uuid "f5d164c1-cdee-5643-a16e-3824d4c07a10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d0752bb-bd77-5627-a153-d3ceecd28fb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411020} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0ff40c2-b221-5f65-9fd3-02b3e136bbd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c7ec246a-1c3b-5830-ae08-ed99cbcc20da" :question/math-academy-id "q-182503" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A⇒B≡¬A∨B$\n- $¬(A⇒B)≡A∨¬B$\n- $¬(A⇒B)≡A∧¬B$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A⇒B$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬A∨B.$\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement II is false. From part I, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nA⇒B≡¬A∨B\n$$\n\nBy negating this statement, we get\n\n$$\n¬(A⇒B)≡¬(¬A∨B).\n$$\n\nDistributing the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law, we have\n\n$$\n¬(¬A∨B)≡¬(¬A)∧¬B.\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n\n$$\nA∧¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, $¬(A⇒B)$ is equivalent to $A∧¬B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411415 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157237 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8479a60b-b5db-5ad4-8100-fd2db8002091" :knowledge-point/title "Using the x R y Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d51ed98-ac24-5fc6-a3ec-b6f3ee2ab3da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411306} {:answer/id #uuid "7a3dd6bc-4ebf-5239-844d-55920fd64463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411331} {:answer/id #uuid "843aa144-29e1-5a7a-b5ed-0a15c64b58f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411337} {:answer/id #uuid "de98701f-4772-5961-b734-93e00e6687bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411364} {:answer/id #uuid "f09ba51b-bb92-5a76-b8d4-9b2465b49c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411370}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a3dd6bc-4ebf-5239-844d-55920fd64463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411331} :answer-field/id #uuid "6eed9603-46c9-5a60-91ed-069b81019517" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5fbac889-73d0-5b8f-a301-da4ef8cb327a" :question/math-academy-id "q-213127" :question/problem "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={1,3}\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x=y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $1\\,R\\,1$\n- $1\\,R\\,3$\n- $3\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,3$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x=y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $=$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $\\ne $ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is  true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $=$, we have $1=1,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement II is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $=,$ we have $1=3,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement III is false. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $\\ne ,$ we have $3\\ne 3,$ which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411416 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157237 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8479a60b-b5db-5ad4-8100-fd2db8002091" :knowledge-point/title "Using the x R y Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "239771eb-1873-5866-a462-6727c30f35e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411301} {:answer/id #uuid "4b2c6f96-fc4e-5564-95d5-c89c5d1f7d0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411312} {:answer/id #uuid "d26ca1a3-71a4-578b-b77f-cb0242ad66b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411361} {:answer/id #uuid "db9ed992-4a83-51d6-999e-2f07e0541fbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411363} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a71d76-19a3-532b-ba22-51c4aa5690b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d26ca1a3-71a4-578b-b77f-cb0242ad66b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411361} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce8e8452-662e-5d96-814f-930fa128fbd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "77fae439-40b7-5f50-94ed-61df5583b377" :question/math-academy-id "q-213144" :question/problem "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={2,4,6}\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x∤y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $2\\,R\\,4$\n- $4\\,R\\,6$\n- $2\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,6$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x∤y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $∤$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $∣$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∤$, we have $2∤4,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement II is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∤,$ we have $4∤6,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is true. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $∣,$ we have $2∣6,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411423 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157237 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8479a60b-b5db-5ad4-8100-fd2db8002091" :knowledge-point/title "Using the x R y Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fb48062-390e-5f50-95e5-46245a4669c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411325} {:answer/id #uuid "862baade-6c89-5f3f-a85b-756cfdc43c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411339} {:answer/id #uuid "8868f605-fc4c-52a6-a072-da3b71bc8621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411340} {:answer/id #uuid "d231e4f4-a503-5b71-aee4-7e628eb0ceaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411360} {:answer/id #uuid "df350fa9-2809-583c-a268-f8b8d3e8def1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fb48062-390e-5f50-95e5-46245a4669c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411325} :answer-field/id #uuid "5deadd58-de08-5459-bea5-ad5b2c6a1739" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3479154-8b81-59b7-8f1a-b3765342f61c" :question/math-academy-id "q-213145" :question/problem "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={1,2}\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x∣y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $1\\,R\\,1$\n- $1\\,R\\,2$\n- $2\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,2$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x∣y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $∣$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $∤$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∣$, we have $1∣1,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement II is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∣,$ we have $1∣2,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is false. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $∤,$ we have $2∤2,$ which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157237 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8479a60b-b5db-5ad4-8100-fd2db8002091" :knowledge-point/title "Using the x R y Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03cf3dd2-1d1e-571a-b1f1-4ac972be63d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411287} {:answer/id #uuid "256131e7-1b92-545f-8144-d6cc2b71dbde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411302} {:answer/id #uuid "744c2bf9-2be2-5a86-9e71-395056b3825f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411326} {:answer/id #uuid "f21c9568-89f5-550c-aeca-3135fd1e4015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411372} {:answer/id #uuid "fd3906e4-2bc8-51c1-b715-a02b38473796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd3906e4-2bc8-51c1-b715-a02b38473796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411381} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9c640c9-599a-5e63-a2a4-02ba3cb94fbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fbdd486a-767d-532e-973f-5e22a4525b04" :question/math-academy-id "q-212638" :question/problem "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={e,\\pi }\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x\\ne y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $e\\,R\\,e$\n- $e\\,R\\,\\pi $\n- $\\pi \\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,\\pi $" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x\\ne y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $\\ne $ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $=$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $\\ne $, we have $e\\ne e,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement II is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $\\ne ,$ we have $e\\ne \\pi ,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is true. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $=,$ we have $\\pi =\\pi ,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}]}
